I18N.register("en", { "help.title": "How to play", "help.body": "<h3>Controls</h3>\n<div class=\"card\"><div class=\"kv\">\n<b>Aim</b><span>Drag anywhere on the table to rotate the cue around the cue ball. Fine angle controls adjust by 0.05°.</span>\n<b>Power</b><span>Drag the power bar: left for gentle shots, right for stronger shots.</span>\n<b>Shoot</b><span>Press Shoot to strike. Aiming and adjusting power never fire a shot.</span>\n<b>Follow / stop / draw</b><span>Follow hits above center so the cue ball moves forward after contact. Stop holds it near the contact point on a straight shot. Draw hits below center to pull it back. The white guide shows the predicted direction. Press the same preset again to cancel. Keys: 1 Follow, 2 Stop, 3 Draw, 0 Clear.</span>\n<b>Jump shot</b><span>Press Jump (key 4) to fly over obstructing balls. The yellow ring marks the landing point. More power means a longer jump, about 130–460 units. The ball loses some speed on landing and cannot use follow, stop or draw at the same time. The first ball contacted after landing must still be legal.</span>\n<b>Spin</b><span>Open Spin and tap a point on the cue ball. Top adds follow, bottom adds draw, and side spin changes rail rebounds. Center is suitable for most shots.</span>\n<b>Ball in hand</b><span>After an opponent fouls, drag the cue ball to a free spot. A green outline is legal; red is illegal.</span>\n<b>Keyboard</b><span>Left/right adjust aim (Shift for finer steps), up/down adjust power, and Space or Enter shoots. The mouse wheel also adjusts power.</span>\n</div></div>\n<h3>Simple 9-Ball</h3><p>Always hit the lowest-numbered ball first. Legally potting the 9 wins, including on the break. A 9 potted on a foul is respotted and does not win.</p>\n<h3>8-Ball</h3><ul>\n<li>The first legally potted ball after the break assigns solids (1–7) or stripes (9–15).</li>\n<li>The break must pot a ball or send at least four balls to a rail. After an illegal break, the opponent may accept the table or request a re-rack.</li>\n<li>If the 8 is potted on the break, the breaker may re-rack or respot the 8 and continue.</li>\n<li>Clear your group before shooting the 8, then call its pocket. Potting the 8 early, in the wrong pocket or on a foul loses the game.</li>\n<li>A foul gives the opponent ball in hand. Balls potted on that shot stay down.</li></ul>\n<h3>14-1 Continuous</h3><p>Call a ball and its pocket before each shot. Potting it in the called pocket scores one point for each ball potted and lets you continue. Otherwise potted balls are respotted and the turn passes. Declare Safety before shooting to pass without scoring. A foul costs one point and gives the opponent ball in hand. First to 30 wins. When one object ball remains, the other 14 are re-racked with the apex empty. No call is required on the break. Kids mode has no calls or foul penalties.</p>\n<h3>Fouls</h3><ul><li>Cue ball scratch</li><li>No object-ball contact</li><li>Wrong first contact</li><li>No ball potted and no rail contact after contact</li><li>Online shot clock expires</li></ul><p>After a foul, the turn passes and the opponent can place the cue ball in a free position.</p>\n<h3>CPU difficulty</h3><p>Kids has larger pockets and no fouls. Easy, Normal, Hard and Mythic progressively improve accuracy, position and foul avoidance. Free practice lets you play without an opponent.</p>\n<h3>Online play</h3><p>Online games are for two human players. Create a room and invite a friend. Others may spectate, up to 20 people. Reconnect within 30 seconds after a dropped connection to continue; otherwise the opponent wins. A room closes when no human players remain.</p>\n" });

